## The hazard this exists to prevent

Q1 v3 (width **16**, digest
`3850d23fa9cd5a5c2744e60c57e103766ecc73cf46cdf8755382e61e3aaafc01`) derives C3 width **240** —
**identical to Q1 v1**. But its 16 fields carry different semantics: Q1 v2's order plus
`decision_boundary_log_nominal_gain_db`.

**`panel-q1v1` would accept a Q1-v3 checkpoint and score it with no error**, because the scorer
derives width from checkpoint weights and compares it against whatever the panel contains — it
consults **no declared schema constant** (`SCORING-PANEL-FIX-2026-09-10.md`,
`/home/sat/mcrl-v025-panelfix-ws`). **Four earlier width defects (236/237/240/296) all failed
loudly. This one fails silently.**

## What to build

**`panel-q1v3`**: the same 20-anchor development panel, C1/C2/C3 state vectors encoded at the
Q1-v3 semantics, verified against a real v3 checkpoint rather than assumed.

**Reuse, do not reimplement.** `PANELFIX2` (`/home/sat/mcrl-v025-panelfix-ws`) and `PANELZ`
(`/home/sat/mcrl-v025-panelz-ws`) built three working panels; **read them, do not write into
them**. Inherit two things specifically:

1. the builder's **fail-closed on width mismatch** — the earlier version truncated the six-field
   global suffix to three and produced the bogus 237;
2. `PANELZ`'s replacement of suffix-prefix slicing with **exact equality**.

Same 20 anchors, global prefix `000-019`, same common catalogue, full-buffer outcomes,
certified fixed point, same-budget anytime incumbent, `coordinator_budget_s == 10.0`,
non-implicit `fixed_point_traversal_order`. **Only the state encoding changes.**

**Also build `panel-q1v3-control`** if the `v3_control` view's encoding differs from `v3`'s in
any way that the scorer would see. If it does not differ, say so and use one panel for both.

